Subject: [overlay 0041] a shadow light must be culled by what it LIGHTS, not by where it sits

Reported in the headset on rerelease data with per-pixel lighting on: "as you pass through
certain areas, dynamic lighting will make portions of floors or walls dark. As you transition
it might even be light in one eye, dark in the other. Before it's light in both, after it's
dark in both." That is a hard step in the lighting of surfaces that never left view — not a
fade, and not the per-eye stamp alias 0040 fixed (this one ends up dark in BOTH eyes).

The mechanism, end to end:

  1. A rerelease shadow light is a plain point ENTITY. subprojects/rerelease-game/rerelease/
     g_misc.cpp setup_dynamic_light sets s.renderfx = RF_CASTSHADOW, a ZERO bbox, and no
     SVF_NOCULL. Its parameters travel separately as CS_SHADOWLIGHTS configstrings, parsed
     into cl.shadowdefs[] by CL_LoadShadowLight.
  2. The SERVER decides per frame whether the client is told about that entity
     (src/server/entities.c): entities without SVF_NOCULL are dropped when their area is not
     connected or they are not visible, and an RF_CASTSHADOW entity is tested against the
     client's PHS. PHS is broader than PVS but still asks about the LIGHT'S OWN LEAF — a
     zero-size point that is usually stuck in a ceiling recess. Walk into a leaf whose PHS
     does not include it and the light stops being sent.
  3. The CLIENT then skips any shadow light whose entity is not in the current server frame
     (CL_AddShadowLights: `if (ent->serverframe != cl.frame.number) continue;`).

So the wall the light is illuminating stays in full view while the light that lights it is
dropped, in one frame, at a leaf boundary. Both eyes see it, because the cull happens once per
server frame; the transient per-eye version appears when a head pose has the two eyes
straddling the boundary across a frame pair.

The fix has two halves, because either alone is wrong:

  A. Server: never position-cull an RF_CASTSHADOW entity (treat it like SVF_NOCULL in that one
     condition). Shadow lights are static and delta-compressed, so after the first send they
     cost essentially nothing per frame. SVF_NOCLIENT is untouched: the game still switches
     lights on and off with it, and the client still honours the serverframe test, so a
     switched-off light still goes away.

  B. Client: cull by RELEVANCE instead. base1 alone defines 143 shadow lights against the
     64-slot MAX_DLIGHTS cap, so "send them all" without a client cull would just move the
     arbitrary drop into the eviction rescan. A light is relevant iff its bounding sphere
     (the cone bounding sphere for a spot, as the renderer computes it) touches at least one
     leaf whose cluster is in the view leaf's PVS — the question the server could not ask,
     because only the client has the light's RADIUS. Every failure mode is conservative: no
     map, no vis, view origin in solid, or a leaf list overflow all admit the light.
     Irrelevance must be proven.

  C. At the cap, 0039's eviction now ranks by intensity x lightstyle x fade x PROXIMITY,
     where proximity = min(1, (radius / max(dist_to_view, radius))^2) — 1 while the viewer is
     inside the light's own radius, inverse-square beyond it. Without it the 64 kept lights
     are the brightest anywhere in the PVS, so a distant powerful light can hold a slot while
     a dim one at the player's feet is dropped, which is exactly the step that is visible.
     The proximity term is a RANKING key only: it lives in a parallel r_shadowlight_rank[]
     array, never in dl->intensity, which is the value the shader renders with (folding it in
     would have dimmed distant lights instead of merely deprioritising them). V_AddLight's
     non-shadow entries still can never be evicted, as in 0039.

  D. Instrument: V_AddLightEx returns whether the light got a slot, and CL_AddShadowLights
     prints, under the existing gl_lightdiag cvar (0040),
     `SHADOWDIAG total= sentbyserver= relevant= admitted= evicted=`.

Nothing in the per-pixel shader, the dlight mask, or 0031/0039/0040's stamp logic is touched.
Both halves are upstream defects and upstreamable as-is; nothing here is stereo-specific — a
flat desktop takes the same path and steps the same way, it just has one eye to notice it with.

